---
import BaseLayout from "../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const title = "Projects";
---

<BaseLayout title={title}>
  <h1>Projects</h1>

  <p>
    Selected side projects I build and maintain. Find more on
    <a href="https://github.com/gildesmarais/" target="_blank" rel="noopener"
      >my GitHub profile</a
    >.
  </p>

  <section id="html2rss">
    <h2><span>HTML</span><span>2</span><span>RSS</span></h2>
    <p>
      <em>Generate RSS feeds from any site.</em>
    </p>

    <p>
      A set of tools that automatically discovers articles on a webpage and
      scrapes their content — including JavaScript-rendered pages via headless
      browsers — to generate reliable RSS feeds.
    </p>

    <h3>Capabilities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Find articles automatically</strong> — detects and extracts items
        without manual selector setup.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Handle JavaScript-heavy sites</strong> — uses headless browsers to
        render and scrape dynamic content.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Normalize feed entries</strong> — unify titles, authors, dates, and
        images.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Fail gracefully</strong> — built-in error handling and monitoring
        hooks.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Publish automatically</strong> — rolling releases keep feeds updated
        without manual deploys.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Serve at scale</strong> — lightweight HTTP service delivers feeds
        on demand.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Who it’s for:</strong> Content teams, researchers, and OSS maintainers
      needing stable feeds from sites without RSS or with dynamic frontends.
    </p>

    <p>
      See the <a
        href="https://html2rss.github.io/"
        target="_blank"
        rel="external nofollow noopener">project website</a
      >. Components:
      <a
        href="https://github.com/html2rss/html2rss"
        target="_blank"
        rel="external nofollow noopener">Ruby gem</a
      >,
      <a
        href="https://github.com/html2rss/html2rss-web"
        target="_blank"
        rel="external nofollow noopener">web service</a
      >,
      <a
        href="https://github.com/html2rss/html2rss-configs"
        target="_blank"
        rel="external nofollow noopener">feed configs repository</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>

  <hr />

  <section id="jekyll-loading-lazy">
    <h2>jekyll-loading-lazy</h2>
    <p>
      <em
        >Drop-in lazy loading for Jekyll sites — zero JS, instant performance
        gains.</em
      >
    </p>

    <p>
      Jekyll plugin that adds <code>loading="lazy"</code> to <code
        >&lt;img&gt;</code
      > and <code>&lt;iframe&gt;</code> at build time—no JavaScript required.
    </p>

    <h3>Capabilities</h3>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Add lazy-loading automatically</strong> — injects attributes into
        every image and iframe.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Skip manual edits</strong> — works without touching templates or
        content files.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Drop JavaScript dependencies</strong> — removes the need for extra
        lazy-load scripts.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>Boost performance & SEO</strong> — improve Core Web Vitals with optimized
        HTML.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <p>
      <strong>Who it’s for:</strong> Developers running Jekyll sites who want effortless
      performance optimization without touching templates or adding JavaScript.
    </p>

    <p>
      Read the <a href="/posts/loading-images-lazily-with-jekyll/"
        >introduction post</a
      >, browse the <a
        href="https://github.com/gildesmarais/jekyll-loading-lazy"
        target="_blank"
        rel="external nofollow noopener">source code on GitHub</a
      >, or install from <a
        href="https://rubygems.org/gems/jekyll-loading-lazy"
        target="_blank"
        rel="external nofollow noopener">RubyGems</a
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
